== What we implemented ==

Alarm (waitUntil, timerInterrupt, cancel)
  Waiting threads are kept in a priority queue ordered by wake-up time.
  waitUntil returns immediately for x <= 0; otherwise it adds the current
  thread to the queue and sleeps with interrupts disabled. timerInterrupt
  readies every thread whose wake-up time has passed, in time order, and
  keeps the original yield(). cancel finds the thread's entry, removes it
  after the search loop (to avoid modifying the queue while iterating),
  and readies the thread; it returns false if the thread has no timer.

KThread.join / finish
  Each thread records whether join has been called on it and which thread
  is waiting. join asserts on self-join and on a second join, returns
  immediately if the target has finished, and otherwise sleeps. finish
  readies the waiting thread, if any.

Condition2 (sleep, wake, wakeAll, sleepFor)
  Implemented with interrupt disable/restore instead of semaphores.
  sleep adds the thread to a wait list and releases the lock atomically
  before sleeping. sleepFor uses Alarm.waitUntil for the timeout and marks
  the waiter as timed. wake uses Alarm.cancel on timed waiters; if cancel
  fails (the timeout already fired), wake skips that waiter and wakes the
  next one, so a wake is never spent on a thread that already timed out.

Rendezvous (exchange)
  Each Rendezvous instance has one Lock and a HashMap from tag to a
  per-tag object holding a value slot, a state (empty / one thread
  waiting / exchange in progress), and two condition variables. The first
  thread stores its value and sleeps; the second swaps values and wakes
  it. Threads that arrive while an exchange is still in progress wait in
  a while loop on a second condition variable until the first thread has
  picked up its value, so an in-progress exchange is never overwritten.
  Per-tag objects are created on first use while holding the lock. No
  thread references are stored outside locks and condition variables.

== How well it worked ==

All of our tests pass, and we ran them with several different -s seeds.

Gradescope public tests: all 6 pass.

== How we tested ==

- Alarm: alarmTest1 (course sample); alarmTest2 (0 and negative waits
  return immediately); alarmTest3 (multiple threads with different
  durations wake in time order); alarmTest4 (cancel on waiting, expired,
  and non-waiting threads).
- join: joinTest1-4 (target already finished, target still running,
  independent pairs, one thread joining several in a row, joins between
  non-parent threads). joinTest5/6 (self-join, double join) trigger
  asserts by design, so they are commented out and run one at a time.
- Condition2: the course InterlockTest and cvTest5, plus sleepForTest1-5
  (timeout with no wake, early wake, mixing sleep and sleepFor with
  wake/wakeAll, wake skipping a waiter whose timeout already fired,
  short timeouts mixed with wake/wakeAll).
- Rendezvous: our test is in Rendezvous.selfTest(). It forks 120
  threads across 4 tags and 3 Rendezvous instances; after
  joining all of them it checks that every thread received another
  thread's value, that pairs are mutual, and that partners used the same
  tag and the same instance. We ran it with several -s seeds and printed the 
  pairings once to confirm that some threads arrive while an exchange is still in
  progress (they get paired out of arrival order), so the waiting path
  for that case is exercised.

